# PHPStan configuration.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# The first full-codebase sweep reported 812 findings. Every one of them was an
# artefact of running PHPStan without telling it what WordPress is and without
# telling it to leave the test shims alone. Read that number as "the analyser
# was not configured", not "the plugin has 812 defects".
#
# The 812 broke down into two families, and both came from the same root:
#
#   574  "Function esc_attr not found." and friends.
#        PHPStan has no WordPress. Every call into core looked undefined.
#
#   ~89  "Function wp_mail invoked with 4 parameters, 3 required."
#        Worse than noise -- confidently wrong. The analyser was reading the
#        deliberately minimal shims the test suites define to run without a
#        WordPress install (tests/php-operational-completeness-smoke.php
#        declares wp_mail($to,$subject,$message); real wp_mail takes five).
#        So it measured production calls against a stub signature and called
#        the correct ones broken.
#
# A report where everything is wrong is worse than no report: nobody reads the
# 813th line to find the one that matters.
#
# This config fixes the second family and only the second family. Scoping the
# analysis to the plugin and excluding the shims removes the ~89 confidently
# wrong findings, which were the actively harmful ones -- they accused correct
# code. The 574 "not found" remain: silencing them needs real WordPress
# signatures from php-stubs, and that means a composer.json plus a committed
# lock file, which the supply-chain gate requires and which cannot be generated
# here. Left as the next step rather than faked, and PHPStan stays advisory
# until it is done.
#
# What this is expected to surface once configured
# ------------------------------------------------
# Genuine argument-count mistakes. That family is not hypothetical here:
# recover_stale_tasks() built an UPDATE with six placeholders and passed five
# arguments, so wpdb::prepare() returned '' and stale leases were never
# recovered -- silently, for the life of the function. tests/validate-prepare-
# arity.py now catches that specific shape inside SQL strings; PHPStan is the
# general case for PHP calls.

parameters:
  level: 1

  paths:
    - prstudio-unified-control

  # The test suites define stand-ins for WordPress functions so they can run
  # without a WordPress install. Those signatures are intentionally partial.
  # Analysing them alongside the plugin is what produced the confidently wrong
  # findings described above.
  excludePaths:
    - tests
    - vendor
    - '*/node_modules/*'

  # The plugin is loaded by WordPress, which defines these before any plugin
  # file runs. PHPStan sees only the plugin, so they have to be declared.
  bootstrapFiles:
    - tests/phpstan-bootstrap.php

  treatPhpDocTypesAsCertain: false
  reportUnmatchedIgnoredErrors: false
